# Requirement format

How to write one technical requirement, in every section of a `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`. Read it
before drafting the first section; the grill and Dev QA check each row against it.

## One-liner

`[While <state>,] [When <trigger>,] <subject> shall <response> <measure>.`

- **Subject**: the product or a part of it by its glossary term or role (the export, the order
  store, the payment provider's client); never a class, file or library.
- **State, trigger**: optional, observable conditions (While an import runs; When a user exports
  up to 100 000 orders). For an unwanted event: `If <event>, then <subject> shall <response>`.
- **Response**: what the system achieves or guarantees, not how.
- **Measure**: statistic, threshold and unit, plus the conditions it holds under (load, data size,
  environment, where it is measured): `p95 ≤ 300 ms at 50 requests/s, measured at the server`.

One requirement per row: split an "and" whose parts can fail separately.

## Table

Every section that holds requirements uses one table with these columns, rows sorted by ID:

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

| Column | Rule |
|---|---|
| ID | `<PREFIX>-<CODE>-<n>` with the catalog code it serves (`SYS-` in the baseline); a reworded row keeps its ID |
| Sub-characteristic | the ISO name from the quality attributes reference (Time behaviour); for an Extra code, a short topic (Alerts, Rollback, Retention) |
| Requirement | the one-liner without its measure, which goes to Measure; it ends with `(refines SYS-<CODE>-n)` when it applies or tightens a baseline row, `(places <scenario ID>)` for a UI placement |
| Measure | the number with its conditions, or the yes/no rule |
| Verify by | `test` automated test · `bench` load or performance run · `static` lint, architecture rule, dependency or secret scan · `inspect` a reviewer reads code or configuration (say what to look for) · `ops` only production shows it (Dev QA checks that the alert, SLO or budget alarm exists) |
| Priority | `Must`: the task is not done until it holds · `Should`: wanted; may give way only through a trade-off the user chose, and a miss is reported, never hidden |
| Source | `user` stated or confirmed by the user · `existing` current behavior, contract or configuration the user confirmed as the requirement · `baseline` the value comes from the SYS row the Requirement names, applied to this feature · `proposed` your suggestion: it becomes `user` only after explicit confirmation, and acceptance is blocked while one remains |

A feature never loosens a baseline row silently: a weaker target is an exception the user approves
and the Trade-offs list records.

## Quality attribute scenario (SEI)

For failure, attack, peak-load and change situations a one-liner hides the conditions. Keep the
row (short Requirement, the response measure in Measure) and add the six parts of the scenario of
Bass, Clements and Kazman as one line under the table:

| Part | Answer |
|---|---|
| Source | who or what causes it: user, attacker, dependency, operator, developer |
| Stimulus | the event: fault, burst of requests, attack, change request |
| Environment | the conditions: normal, peak, degraded, during a deployment |
| Artifact | what is affected: a capability, a store, an interface, the code base |
| Response | what the system does |
| Response measure | how the response is judged: time, count, share, effort |

`CHK-REL-2`: source payment provider · stimulus no answer within 3 s · environment normal
operation at peak load · artifact placing an Order · response the Order stays unpaid and the
Customer is told that payment is pending (CHK-5.3) · response measure answer within 5 s, 0
duplicate charges.

A change scenario measures effort or reach: `CHK-MAINT-3`: source developer · stimulus a new
payment method · environment development · artifact the payment capability · response added with
its tests · response measure no checkout rule changes.

## Vague words

Beyond the adjectives the grill rejects, these fail unless a number or a yes/no rule follows:
efficient, flexible, easy, simple, quick, responsive, real-time, highly available, minimal, optimal,
adequate, appropriate, reasonable, as needed, if possible, where applicable, etc., and/or, best
practice, industry standard, state of the art, and "supports" (supports large files: how large?).

## Verifiable without a number

Allowed when anyone can answer yes or no:
- An absolute rule: every write is authorized on the server side; no personal data in logs.
- Conformance to a named standard and level: WCAG 2.2 AA; OWASP ASVS level 2 for sign-in.
- The presence of a capability or artifact: an alert exists for failed payments; every action is
  reachable by keyboard; a rollback is one command.
- A structural rule a tool can check: checkout rules depend on nothing in the payment provider's
  client.

## Long-lived and decision-relevant

The grill's Long-lived and Decision-relevant questions apply to every row. Typical rewrites:

| Fails | Why | Rewrite |
|---|---|---|
| Orders are cached in Redis for 60 s | names the implementation | Repeated reads of an order within 60 s answer in p95 ≤ 50 ms |
| The order export service streams rows | a component and the how | An export of 1 000 000 rows uses at most 256 MB of memory |
| Uses the HTTP client library in version 4 | a library version | drop it, or a `CON-n` when a counterpart requires it |
| The system logs errors | true of any system; changes no choice | When an export fails, the failure is recorded with its cause and alerts after 3 in 10 min |
| Must be fast | adjective | p95 ≤ 300 ms at 50 requests/s |
| Currently takes 4 s; must improve | describes today's code, goes stale | p95 ≤ 1 s at 20 requests/s |
| The new export button | change-relative word | The export action appears in the order list (places ORD-2.1) |

## Examples

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ORD-PERF-1 | Time behaviour | When a user exports up to 100 000 orders, the download shall start | p95 ≤ 2 s to first byte | bench | Must | user |
| ORD-PERF-2 | Time behaviour | Order search shall answer within the baseline target at its own peak load (refines SYS-PERF-1) | p95 ≤ 500 ms at 30 requests/s | bench | Should | baseline |
| ORD-SEC-1 | Confidentiality | An export shall contain only orders of the requester's tenant | 0 foreign rows | test | Must | existing |
| ORD-REL-2 | Recoverability | If the order store is lost, orders shall be restored from a backup | RPO ≤ 5 min, RTO ≤ 1 h | ops | Must | user |
| ORD-IC-1 | Operability | Every export action shall be reachable by keyboard alone | yes/no | inspect | Should | user |
